# {{non-gloss|Senses relating to a thin, pointed object.}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} A [[sharp#Adjective|sharp]] [[stick#Noun|stick]] used for [[write|writing]] on [[clay]] [[tablet]]s or other [[surface#Noun|surfaces]]; a [[stylus]]; {{lb|en|by extension|obsolete}} an [[instrument]] used to write with [[ink#Noun|ink]]; a [[pen#Noun|pen]].
##* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|chapter=Palamon and Arcite|book=II|page=26|passage=Thus while his Thoughts the lingring Day beguile, / To gentle ''Arcite'' let us turn our '''Style'''; [...]}}
## A [[tool#Noun|tool]] with a sharp [[point#Noun|point]] used in [[engraving#Noun|engraving]]; a [[burin]], a [[graver#Noun|graver]], a [[stylet]], a stylus.
##* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:James Townley (Methodist)|James Townley]]|chapter=I|title=Illustrations of Biblical Literature, Exhibiting the History and Fate of the Sacred Writings, from the Earliest Period to the Present Century;{{nb...|Including Biographical Notices of Translators, and Other Eminent Biblical Scholars.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed [by B. Crompton] for [[w:Longman|Longman, Hurst, Rees, Orme, and Brown]],{{nb...|Paternoster Row.}}|year=1821|volume=I|section=part I (From the Giving of the Law to the Birth of Christ)|page=27|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/illustrationsofb01town/page/27/mode/1up|oclc=498430079|passage=From [[Job]] xix. 24. it appears to have been usual in his day, to write or engrave upon Plates of {{smallcaps|Lead}}, which might easily be done with a ''Pen'', or ''Graver'', or '''''Style''' of Iron'', or other hard metal.}}
## The [[gnomon]] or [[pin#Noun|pin]] of a [[sundial]], the [[shadow#Noun|shadow]] of which [[indicate]]s the [[hour]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Joseph Moxon|chapter=Operat[ioni] II. To Describe a Dyal upon a Horizontal Plane.|title=Mechanick Dyalling: Teaching any Man, though of an Ordinary Capacity and Unlearned in Mathematicks, to Draw a True Sun-dial on any Given Plane,{{nb...|however Scituated: Only with the Help of a Straight Ruler and a Pair of Compasses; and without any Arithmetical Calculation.}}|edition=3rd|location=London|publisher=Printed for James Moxon,{{nb...|at the sign of the Atlas in Warwick-Lane.}}|year=1697|page=17|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xfRbAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA17|oclc=57050730|passage=Laſt of all fit a Triangular Iron, whoſe angular point being laid to the Center of the ''Dyal Plane'', one ſide muſt agree with the ''Subſtilar Line'', and its other ſide with the ''Stilar Line''; ſo is the '''''Stile''''' made. And this '''''Stile''''' you muſt erect perpendicularly over the ''Subſtilar Line'' on the ''Dyal Plane'', and there fix it. Then is your ''Dyal'' finiſhed.}}
