#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Conyers Middleton|chapter=To the Right Honorable [[w:John Hervey, 2nd Baron Hervey|John Lord Hervey]], Lord Keeper of His Majesty’s Privy Seal|title=The History of the Life of M. Tullius Cicero|edition=new|location=Basel|publisher=Printed for J. J. Tourneisen [''i.e.'', Johann Jakob Thurneysen]; and J. L. Legrand|year=1790|volume=I|page=iii|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ZuM0j2LKvZoC&pg=PR3|oclc=938165873|passage=The public will naturally expect, that in chuſing a Patron for ''the Life of'' [[w:Cicero|{{smallcaps|Cicero}}]], I should addreſs myſelf to ſome perſon of illuſtrious rank, diſtinguished by his parts and eloquence, and bearing a principal share in the great affairs of the Nation; who, according to the uſual '''ſtyle''' of Dedications, might be the proper ſubject of a compariſon with the Hero of my piece.}}
