##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Joseph Moxon|chapter=§&nbsp;25. The Office of the Warehouse-keeper. [(As an Appendix.) Ancient Customs Used in a Printing-house.]|title=Mechanick Exercises: Or, The Doctrine of Handy-books. Applied to the Art of Printing|volume=II|location=London|publisher=Printed for Joseph Moxon{{nb...|on the west-side of Fleet-ditch, at the sign of the Atlas.}}|year=1683|section=number XXII|page=356|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=npQtAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA356|oclc=427106359|passage=Every ''Printing-houſe'' is by the Cuſtom of Time out of mind, called a ''Chappel''; and all the Workmen that belong to it are ''Members of the Chappel'': and the Oldeſt Freeman is the ''Father of the Chappel''. I ſuppoſe the '''ſtile''' was originally conferred upon it by the courteſie of ſome great Churchman, or men, (doubtleſs when Chappels were in more veneration than of late years they have been here in ''England'') who for the Books of Divinity that proceeded from a ''Printing-houſe'', gave it the Reverend Title of ''Chappel''.}}
