#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Joshua Reynolds|chapter=Discourse IV. Delivered at the Royal Academy.|title=Discourses on Painting and the Fine Arts, Delivered at the Royal Academy|location=London|publisher=Printed for Jones and Co.,{{nb...|3, Acton Place, Kingsland Road.}}|year=1825|page=23|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=axv4PRecQIEC&pg=PA23|column=1|oclc=1063550111|passage=[T]here are two distinct '''styles''' in history painting; the grand, and the splendid or ornamental. The great '''style''' stands alone, and does not require, perhaps does not so well admit, any addition from inferior beauties. The ornamental '''style''' also possesses its own peculiar merit. However, though the union of the two may make a sort of composite '''style''', yet that '''style''' is likely to be more imperfect than either of those which goes to its composition.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Allan Cunningham (author)|Allan Cunningham]]|chapter=XI|title=The Life of Sir [[w:David Wilkie (artist)|David Wilkie]]; {{...|with His Journals, Tours, and Critical Remarks on Works of Art; and a Selection from His Correspondence.}} In Three Volumes|location=London|publisher=[[w:John Murray (publisher)|John Murray]],{{nb...|Albemarle Street.}}|year=1843|volume=II|page=472|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KT06AAAAcAAJ&pg=PA472|oclc=297154957|passage=To our English tastes it is unnecessary to advocate the '''style''' of [[w:Diego Velázquez|[Diego] Velazquez]]. [...] Sir [[w:Joshua Reynolds|Joshua [Reynolds]]], [[w:George Romney (painter)|[George] Romney]], and [[w:Henry Raeburn|[Henry] Raeburn]], whether from imitation or instinct, seem powerfully imbued with his '''style''', and some of our own time, even to our landscape painters, seem to possess the same affinity.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Italian Architecture and Its Various European Offshoots|editor=w:George Godwin|magazine=[[w:Building (magazine)|The Builder. An Illustrated Weekly Magazine for the Architect, Engineer, Archæologist, Constructor, & Art-lover]]|location=London|publisher=Publishing office, York Street, {{w|Covent Garden}}, W.C. [printed by Cox and Wyman]|date=4 April 1863|volume=XXI|issue=1052|page=239|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=s21TAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA239|column=1|oclc=317999157|passage=This '''style''' was sometimes called Palladian from the fact of [[w:Andrea Palladio|[Andrea] Palladio]] having fully developed and absorbed into his own system the '''styles''' of his great predecessors of the [Florentine] school, [...]}}
